        "November to February suit study best. The air is cool, daytime temperatures in Bareilly average around 15 degrees in January, and there is no loo to drain a student's afternoon. The one complication is fog in late December and January, which slows roads and trains and makes evening travel unpleasant; online lessons simply bypass it, provided power holds.",
        "March brings Holi and the end of the school year, then April starts a new session just as the heat begins. May and June are the hardest months: hot winds, long afternoons and power cuts coincide with school exams, the IB May session and Cambridge's May-June series. Lessons work best early or after dusk, with timed papers saved for cooler hours and drafts finished before the worst weeks.",
        "The monsoon arrives around late June, and Sawan in July and August brings Kanwar processions and altered traffic across the region. Low-lying villages along the Deoha can be cut off for a day or two. This is a good stretch for indoor work on Internal Assessment drafts and mock papers, as long as a mobile hotspot stands ready.",
        "Autumn and winter then fill with festivals: Dussehra and Ramlila, Diwali, Eid in its shifting dates, the Uttarayani fair in mid-January, and two long wedding seasons that change family routines. Exams sit over all this: IB in May with results in early July, November retakes, Cambridge in May-June and October-November, Edexcel in January and May-June.",
