  faqs: [
    { question: "Is this page about Nawabganj in Bareilly district?", answer: "Yes: it covers Nawabganj, the tehsil and nagar palika about 30 km north-east of Bareilly. Students in other Uttar Pradesh towns called Nawabganj, in Barabanki, Gonda or Unnao districts, can use the same online service, although the school and board details here refer to the Bareilly town. Lessons run over video, so the address affects only timing." },
    { question: "Does any school in Nawabganj or Bareilly teach the IB or IGCSE?", answer: "We could not confirm one in either place. Schools here follow the UP Board, CBSE or ICSE. The IB and IGCSE schools we could confirm are in Noida, with an IB Diploma school in Lucknow, so most families keep their child in the present school and add an online tutor for the target syllabus." },
    { question: "Will a tutor come to my house in Nawabganj?", answer: "No. Home visits are offered only in Gurugram and parts of Delhi NCR, so nobody comes to Nawabganj. Your child takes online home tuition: a live one-to-one lesson on a laptop from their own room, with the tutor on video and a shared whiteboard, which gives private, focused teaching without a tutor needing to be in the house." },
    { question: "How does matching work for a Nawabganj student?", answer: "You send the programme, subject and level, present grade and the exam session. We then look nationally for tutors who have taught that course recently, because the district has no pool of IB teachers, and put forward one with a short explanation. A free trial lesson follows, and if the fit is poor we propose someone else." },
    { question: "Can my child take IGCSE as a private candidate from Nawabganj?", answer: "Generally yes. You choose Cambridge or Pearson Edexcel, settle the syllabus code and tier, and then ask an exam centre, most likely in Bareilly, Lucknow or Delhi, whether it accepts private entries and by which date. The lessons run online and leave school untouched. Begin the centre enquiry early, because places and deadlines change." },
    { question: "What should I expect to pay for IB or IGCSE tuition?", answer: "There is no published rate card. The figure depends on programme and level, session length and frequency, and the tutor's recent experience with your specification, and you get it in writing before you book. Your address does not change it. The first lesson is free, so nothing is paid until you have watched the teaching at work." },
    { question: "Is the trial lesson free, and what happens after it?", answer: "It is free, with no obligation. The tutor and your child work on a real syllabus topic, and you see how questions are asked and explained. Afterwards you receive a plan for the first month and the written rate, and you decide to continue, adjust the plan or meet a different tutor. Nothing recurring starts until you agree." },
    { question: "Will the tutor do my child's IA or Extended Essay?", answer: "No. A tutor explains the criteria, helps narrow a question, suggests how data might be gathered and comments honestly on drafts the student has written, but never writes, rewrites or supplies any of the assessed work. That would break IB integrity rules and could cost the qualification, so it is a line we do not cross." },
    { question: "My child studies in a Hindi-medium UP Board school. Is the IB realistic?", answer: "It can be, with patience. The first job is written English: building a glossary of technical terms, practising short explanations each week and learning what each command word asks. Students who read Hindi well can also take Hindi A or B. A tutor sets a pace that lets subject knowledge and English strengthen together." },
    { question: "Which IB subjects are available for a Nawabganj student?", answer: "Most Diploma subjects can be matched, including both Maths courses, Physics, Chemistry, Biology, Economics, Business Management, Computer Science, Psychology, Geography, English A, Hindi A and B, and TOK. Hindi A is confirmed when we read your brief. Share subject, level and session, and we will say plainly whether a specialist is available." },
    { question: "Do you teach PYP and MYP students as well?", answer: "Yes. PYP lessons focus on reading, writing, number sense and the research habits behind the Exhibition, in short regular sessions. MYP lessons focus on criterion-based work in sciences, maths and language and literature, and on the Personal Project journal. With no IB school nearby, tutoring often keeps up routines a transferred child would otherwise lose." },
    { question: "What if power or the internet fails during a lesson?", answer: "Message the tutor as soon as possible and reconnect when the supply returns; a mobile hotspot is a worthwhile backup. A missed half hour can usually be recovered by moving another session that week. Summer power cuts and monsoon days are the likeliest causes, so finish drafts and mock papers a few days before any deadline." },
    { question: "When should a Nawabganj student start?", answer: "At the start of the course if possible, meaning Class 11 for the Diploma and Grade 9 for IGCSE. Early months are the easiest time to settle written English, command words and the first Internal Assessment plan. A later start still helps; the tutor then targets the highest-value topics and timed papers for the next exam series." },
    { question: "Can my child prepare for JEE or NEET alongside IB or IGCSE?", answer: "Many students attempt both and manage with a realistic timetable. Maths, Physics, Chemistry and Biology overlap in content, but entrance papers and IB or IGCSE papers are written in different styles, so each needs separate practice. We tutor the IB or IGCSE side; entrance coaching is separate. Check each exam's current eligibility rules for your board." },
    { question: "Is Cambridge or Edexcel more suitable from Nawabganj?", answer: "Choose by practicalities: which board's exam centre, dates and subject list work for you, and whether a future school prefers one. Cambridge sits in May-June and October-November, Edexcel in January and May-June. Both are recognised worldwide. After choosing, keep every past paper and mark scheme from that board so practice matches the exam." },
    { question: "Will Indian universities accept IB or IGCSE results?", answer: "Generally yes, with the right paperwork. The Association of Indian Universities issues equivalence for the IB Diploma and IGCSE-based qualifications, CUET-UG is open to students of every board, and JEE, NEET and UP counselling each set their own conditions. Apply for equivalence early in Class 12 and check every institution's rules directly." },
    { question: "Can lessons pause for Holi, Diwali, Eid and wedding seasons?", answer: "Yes. Most families keep one weekday evening and a weekend slot, and agree with the tutor which festival or wedding days to skip or move. Because lessons are online, an extra session before a mock or an exam series is easy to add, and nobody has to travel to catch up." },
    { question: "What if we want a different tutor?", answer: "Tell us, and we will propose another. Progress is reviewed every few weeks, and a change of tutor is an ordinary part of the service, not a complaint. The free first lesson is meant to expose a poor match early, and because rates are agreed in writing at the start, changing tutor brings no surprises." },
    { question: "Is IB Gram linked to any school or board mentioned on this page?", answer: "No. IB Gram is independent. It has no link with the IB, Cambridge, Pearson or any school, college or university named here, and it does not speak for them. The names appear so that you can see the real options. Admission, exam entry and equivalence are decided by those bodies, not by us." },
  ],

